{"tool": "finish", "summary": "Requests to the REST API and the Google Reader API that write a row in `users` or `feeds` now append to a new `audit_log` table. The build and existing tests pass (53 unit packages, 102 integration tests); nothing exercises the new audit rows yet.\n\n**What was added**\n- **Table:** a new migration creates `audit_log` with `id`, `user_id` (the actor), `action` (`create`/`update`/`delete`), `table_name`, `row_id`, `created_at`. There was no existing table, so this schema is my choice. It has no foreign keys, so audit rows survive deletion of the user or feed.\n- **Storage:** `Storage.AppendAuditLog` in `internal/storage/audit_log.go` writes one row per affected row ID.\n- **REST API (`internal/api`):**\n  - Users: create, update and remove.\n  - Feeds: create, update, remove and single-feed refresh. Refresh is audited even when it fails, because failure paths still update the feed row.\n  - OPML import: one row per feed created, including feeds created before an import fails partway. This uses a new `opml.Handler.ImportFeeds` that returns the created IDs.\n  - Cascades: removing a category or a user also audits each feed deleted with it.\n- **Google Reader API (`internal/googlereader`):** subscribe/quick-add, unsubscribe, rename, move, and disable-tag (the feeds reassigned to another category).\n- **Exception:** `SetLastLogin` in the auth middlewares and client login is not audited.\n- **Policy:** `audit_log` added to the `.agentpolicy` nouns of `storage`, `api` and `googlereader`; `@effects` tags updated on the functions I touched.\n\n**Gaps you should know about**\n- The audit row is written by the handler right after the change, not in the same transaction. A crash or audit-insert failure between the two leaves the change unaudited; an insert failure returns a 500 after the change has already been applied.\n- `PUT /v1/feeds/refresh` and `PUT /v1/categories/{id}/refresh` are not audited. They only queue jobs; the feed rows are written